Operator \eqref{eq:2} is called a Volterra quadratic stochastic operator of a two-sex population (VQSOBP) if
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:3}
\begin{cases}
 p_{ik,j}^{(f)}=0, & \text{if }j\notin\{i,k\},\\
 p_{ik,l}^{(m)}=0, & \text{if }l\notin\{i,k\}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

Let $A_1^{(f)}=A_1^{(m)}=:A_1$, $\operatorname{rank} A_1=2$, and suppose there exists
\[
C=(C_i)_{i\in I_1}\in\ker A_1,\qquad C_i>0.
\]
Then $\Omega_1$ is invariant under system \eqref{eq:5}, and on $\Omega_1$ the system takes the form
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:7}
\dot s_i=s_i(A_1s)_i,\qquad i\in I_1.
\end{equation}
Every trajectory with $s^0\in\Delta$, $A_1s^0\ne0$ is non-constant and periodic.

Let $J$ be a Hurwitz matrix, $-2<\hbar<0$ and
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:15}
4|\hbar|KC_BM_0\le1-|1+\hbar|,
\end{equation}
where
\[
M_0=\sup_{t\ge0}\|e^{Jt}v^0\|.
\]
Then the series
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:16}
u(t)=u^*+\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}v_k(t,\hbar)
\end{equation}
converges absolutely and uniformly on $[0,+\infty)$, and its sum is the unique solution of problem \eqref{eq:10}.

Let $-2<\hbar<0$ and suppose there exists $A\in(0,V_{\max})$, for which
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:30}
\zeta_A<1,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:31}
\kappa\widetilde f(U_0,V_0)\le(1-\zeta_A)A.
\end{equation}
Then, at $p=1$ series \eqref{eq:27}, together with the series of its first and second derivatives, converges absolutely and uniformly on $[0,+\infty)$, and the sum of the series is the unique solution of problem \eqref{eq:24}, \eqref{eq:25}.
